    "difficulty": "Development",
    "difficultyReason": "Parts a and b are trivial: 3! and spotting the one descending number. Part c carries the demand. The student must see that each set of three digits gives exactly one descending arrangement, so the count of descending numbers equals 6C3. This is a reframing from ordered to unordered, and it is only partly scaffolded by the preceding parts and the word 'hence'. It is more than the routine case and complement selections in p57-q6. It is less demanding than p59-q13, whose last part needs a fuller case decision with no signposting.",
